\description{
\ifelse{html}{\href{https://lifecycle.r-lib.org/articles/stages.html#experimental}{\figure{lifecycle-experimental.svg}{options: alt='[Experimental]'}}}{\strong{[Experimental]}}

Solves for a model's steady state using declared tuning strategies. The
built-in defaults hold final recruitment and the resource abundance fixed,
then adjust reproduction parameters and resource capacity. Extensions declare
which states their strategies hold or evolve, what parameters may change,
and how the held targets are balanced. The restored candidate is checked
against every effective equation rate before its final steady assessment.

It is not the function for asking where a model with given parameters
settles. Because the reproduction rate is held at its current value, it
cannot reach a steady state in which a species' reproduction rate is very
different, such as an alternative stable state or a state that exists
because predation changes the reproduction of the prey. Because it
re-derives \code{erepro}/\code{R_max} and \code{cc_pp}, two states found with it do not
share parameters. Use \code{\link[=findSteadyState]{findSteadyState()}} for those questions.
}
\details{
Each provider supplies named strategies and a default. Use \code{strategies} to
choose alternatives, and \code{joint} for declared strategies owning coupled
processes. The selected strategies must cover the whole tuning operation
without overlapping parameter ownership. Missing support, unknown stationary
behaviour and unsupported solver combinations stop before the search.

Recruitment targets come from the complete effective rate pipeline,
including named replacements and extension wrappers. A held target replaces
the final recruitment entering the equations, so wrappers are not applied a
second time to a total that already includes their contribution.

Balancers run once after the constrained search. They must preserve the
candidate state and all effective target equation rates, including growth,
diffusion, total mortality and final recruitment. Mizer independently checks
the released model; it does not repair changed rates by solving the consumers
again or by choosing an undeclared numerical balancing method.

The \code{"convergence"} attribute separates \code{search_assessment}, measured with
the selected state and rate constraints, from \code{assessment}, measured after
restoring and balancing the model. Whole-model assessment is the default.
Select \code{scope = "conditional"} with explicit \code{fixed} owners for a subsystem;
those inputs must also be held by the selected tuning strategies.

Holding those inputs fixed is what makes the search reliable, but it does not
make the result certain: the state that is stored is only as close to a fixed
point as the solver's tolerance allowed, and with \code{solver = "project"} the
run may instead have stopped on a limit cycle or on a species going extinct.
Check the result rather than assuming it; see the section below.
}
\section{Choosing a solver}{
\code{solver = "project"} (the default) runs the dynamics until they settle, via
\code{\link[=projectUntilSettled]{projectUntilSettled()}}, using \code{\link[=distanceMaxRelRDI]{distanceMaxRelRDI()}} as its distance
function. It needs no extra packages. Every required dynamic state must
provide convergence support.

\code{solver = "newton"} solves the steady-state equation directly with a
Newton-type root finder from the \code{nleqslv} package. It converges even when
the steady state is dynamically \strong{unstable}, where the time-stepping solver
cannot, and it discovers the support of the steady state automatically. It
starts from the spectra in \code{initialN(params)}, so a reasonable initial guess
still matters — for example the spectra from a nearby stable
parameterisation, or the (diverging) output of \code{solver = "project"}.

Because the resource is held fixed either way, \code{solver = "newton"} here does
not need the resource to be a semichemostat, unlike in \code{\link[=findSteadyState]{findSteadyState()}}
where the resource is one of the unknowns. The Newton solver does not solve
extension component states; dynamic components require explicit conditional
exclusions with \code{fixed}, or use the projection solver.
}

\section{What you get back may not be a steady state}{


The stopping criterion is a proxy. It says that two states \code{t_per} years
apart differ by less than \code{distance_tol} on whatever scale the criterion is
measured on; it does not say that the state reached is a fixed point. There
are four ways the returned object can fail to be one:
\itemize{
\item the run converged on its own scale while the biomasses are still visibly
drifting (\code{termination = "distance_tolerance"});
\item the run reached \code{t_max} without converging
(\code{termination = "time_limit"});
\item the run settled on a limit cycle (\code{termination = "cycle_detected"}), in
which case the state stored is one point on that cycle;
\item the run stopped because a species was going extinct
(\code{termination = "extinction"}).
}

So treat the result as a claim to be checked rather than as a guarantee:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{attr(params, "convergence")$attractor  # "fixed_point", "limit_cycle" or NA
attr(params, "convergence")$residual   # largest biomass drift, in 1/year
isSteady(params)                       # TRUE if within tolerance
summary(params)                        # includes the biomass-drift verdict
plot(getSteadyResidual(params))        # which species, and at which sizes
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{attractor} is the field that answers the question: it is \code{"fixed_point"}
only where the measured biomass drift is within \code{residual_tol}, so it
cannot be satisfied by a distance function that has merely gone quiet.
\code{termination} says how the run ended and \code{converged} whether the solver met
its own criterion; neither is a claim about the state. The last line says
\emph{where} the model is not steady, which is the one to reach for when it is
not: a model that is off steady state is usually off in one species or one
part of the size range, and the plot names it. See \code{\link[=getSteadyResidual]{getSteadyResidual()}}
for why the verdict is phrased in terms of biomass drift rather than the
largest per-capita rate.

The messages this function prints say the same thing — a converged run
whose biomasses are still moving reports the drift and adds "Reduce the
tolerance on the distance function to converge further." — but they are
suppressed by \code{info_level = 0}, so in a script the \code{"convergence"}
attribute is the reliable check.

Even a genuine fixed point can be one the model cannot sustain. \code{erepro}
is the proportion of the energy invested in reproduction that ends up as
egg biomass, so if a re-derived \code{erepro} is greater than 1, the
reproduction rate held during the search is inconsistent with the state
that was found. Usually this means that the state you asked for does not
exist with births held at their current values; try \code{\link[=findSteadyState]{findSteadyState()}}
instead. mizer warns when this happens, but with the default \code{preserve}
the warning reads the same for an \code{erepro} of 1.01 as for one of 1e34,
and \code{info_level = 0} suppresses it, so look at the values:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{species_params(params)$erepro          # greater than 1 is unrealistic
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Finally, a genuine fixed point need not be a \emph{stable} one. Use
\code{\link[=getStability]{getStability()}} to find out, and \code{solver = "newton"} to converge onto a
fixed point that the dynamics themselves would run away from.
}

